{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. There is no sure outcome (i.e., no outcome with 100% probability) in either lottery, so the reference value is zero. The differences between each outcome and the reference value are simply the outcomes themselves (i.e., -6000 and 0 for Lottery B; -3000 and 0 for Lottery A). The reason evaluates the mean (expected) difference from the reference value, weighted by probabilities. For Lottery A: (0.002 × (-3000 - 0) + 0.998 × (0 - 0)) = -6. For Lottery B: (0.001 × (-6000 - 0) + 0.999 × (0 - 0)) = -6. Thus, both lotteries have the same mean difference (-6 Euros). The decision reason is indifferent between A and B.",
